(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ent-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Advanced ENT History & Examination Quiz 1 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does the canal look like in ACUTE otitis externa?","choices":["Swollen, narrow, moist, pale and tender, and it may be erythematous","Thickened, red and itchy","Dry, scaly and pale, with no tenderness","Normal, with the abnormality confined to the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The swelling and tenderness dominate, and the narrowing is often what makes the examination difficult. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"The Weber test is done and the patient hears the tone only in the LEFT ear. Which ear has sensorineural hearing loss?","choices":["Neither — this is a normal result","Both","The right","The left"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensorineural loss lateralises AWAY from the damaged side, so hearing the tone on the left places the lesion on the right. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"FINE hair and COARSE hair on examination point to which conditions?","choices":["Fine hair to hyperthyroidism; coarse hair to hypothyroidism","Fine hair to hypothyroidism; coarse hair to hyperthyroidism","Both to hypothyroidism","Both to nutritional deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a small finding, but it is examined for at the same moment as the scalp and the skull. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Hard nodules on the auricle made of uric acid crystals. What are they, and what do they indicate?","choices":["Keloids, indicating previous trauma","Cutaneous cysts, indicating follicular obstruction","Exostoses, indicating repeated cold water exposure","Tophi, indicating chronic tophaceous gout"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are deposits rather than growths, and finding them on the ear points to long-standing disease. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does a septal haematoma require URGENT drainage?","choices":["To prevent secondary sinusitis","To prevent necrosis of the septal cartilage","To prevent the blood tracking into the orbit","To prevent airway obstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cartilage has no blood supply of its own — it depends on the lining that the haematoma has stripped away, so the clock starts immediately. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which part of the hand is used to palpate the lymph nodes of the neck?","choices":["The pads of the index and middle fingers","The fingertips of all four fingers","The thumb and index finger in a pincer grip","The ulnar border of the hand"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pads are the most sensitive surface, and two fingers cover a node without rolling past it. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is needed to inspect the POSTERIOR pharynx properly?","choices":["An otoscope with the largest speculum","A nasal speculum","Transillumination in a dark room","A tongue blade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The tongue obscures the posterior wall and has to be depressed out of the way. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Why should recent dental work be asked about when a patient presents with facial pain?","choices":["Dental work is a recognised cause of epistaxis","Dental infection is the commonest cause of frontal sinusitis","Dental work may affect the maxillary sinuses","Dental anaesthetic commonly causes anosmia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The maxillary sinus floor sits directly above the upper tooth roots, so a dental procedure can present as sinus pain. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What causes a PERFORATED nasal septum?","choices":["A foreign body lodged for several days","Repeated epistaxis from Kiesselbach's plexus","Trauma, surgery or drug use","Allergic rhinitis and chronic sinusitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three destroy the septal blood supply or the cartilage itself, and drug use is the one patients do not volunteer. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What does bleeding of the gums suggest?","choices":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Necrotising ulcerative gingivitis only","Oral carcinoma","Gingivitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding gums point to gingivitis — inflammation of the gum margin itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Endemic goitre is associated with what?","choices":["Previous radiation exposure","Iodine deficiency","Autoimmune destruction of the gland","Thyroid-stimulating antibodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endemic means it clusters geographically, and the reason is dietary iodine. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"An abnormally ENLARGED head raises which two conditions?","choices":["Microcephaly and hydrocephalus","Hypothyroidism and acromegaly","Paget disease and microcephaly","Hydrocephalus and Paget disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One expands the skull from within, the other thickens the bone itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A headache of SUDDEN, SEVERE onset should raise which diagnosis?","choices":["Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Tension headache","Migraine","Cluster headache"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sudden and severe is the pattern that cannot be attributed to a benign cause without investigation. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A sore AND SMOOTH tongue suggests what?","choices":["Oral candidiasis","Nutritional deficiency","Aphthous ulceration","Geographic tongue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The smoothness is loss of papillae, and that combination of sore and smooth is the pointer. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is a NORMAL Weber result?","choices":["Heard only through bone conduction","Not heard at all","Midline, or heard equally in both ears","Lateralised to the right"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. There is no lateralisation to report when both sides transmit equally. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A smooth, beefy red tongue points to which deficiency?","choices":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Iron alone","Vitamin D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The appearance is atrophic glossitis, and it earns a look for the rest of the deficiency picture. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A patient says they are “dizzy”. Which four categories must the history separate?","choices":["Orthostatic, cardiac, neurological and metabolic","Vertigo, presyncope, disequilibrium and psychiatric causes","Vertigo, migraine, seizure and syncope","Peripheral vertigo, central vertigo, tinnitus and hearing loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The word means nothing until the patient explains it, and these are the four things it turns out to mean. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two situations can the tuning fork tests NOT distinguish?","choices":["Presbycusis from noise-induced loss","Normal hearing from bilateral sensorineural loss, and normal hearing from mixed conductive and sensorineural loss","Conductive from sensorineural loss","Right-sided from left-sided loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both tests work by comparing sides or routes, so a loss that is symmetrical or that affects both routes leaves the comparison looking normal. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A patient speaks unusually SOFTLY despite a hearing complaint. Which loss does that point to?","choices":["Conductive — the inner ear and cochlear nerve are intact, so the patient hears their own voice normally","Sensorineural — the patient compensates by speaking quietly","Mixed loss only","Presbycusis specifically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and bone conduction of their own voice is if anything enhanced, so they have no reason to speak up. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A firm raised lesion at an old ear piercing site. Which finding is it, and who is most prone to it?","choices":["A cutaneous cyst — more common in darker-skinned individuals","Basal cell carcinoma — more common in darker-skinned individuals","A keloid — scar tissue, more common in darker-skinned individuals","Tophi — and there is no skin-type association"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Keloids are commonest on the shoulders and upper chest but arise at piercing sites, which puts them on the ear. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does the ABSENCE of cough count toward the Centor score rather than its presence?","choices":["Cough is a criterion only in children","A cough points toward a viral cause, so its absence raises the probability of streptococcal infection","Coughing masks tonsillar exudates on examination","A cough indicates the infection has spread to the lower airway"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The rule is built to separate bacterial from viral pharyngitis, and cough is the most useful viral marker in the set. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A diffusely enlarged, FIRM thyroid points to what?","choices":["Graves disease","A multinodular goitre","Thyroid carcinoma","Hashimoto thyroiditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Firmness is the feature that separates it from Graves, where the gland is soft. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Under what symptom duration is acute BACTERIAL sinusitis unlikely?","choices":["Less than twelve weeks","Less than seven days","Less than three days","Less than four weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most cases under a week are viral, which is the single most useful piece of timing in the nose section. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What usually causes a tympanic membrane perforation, and what may accompany it?","choices":["Otitis media, and drainage may come through the perforation","Otitis externa, with itching through the perforation","Barotrauma, with vertigo through the perforation","Cerumen impaction, with pain through the perforation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The perforation is usually the consequence of middle ear disease rather than a primary event. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How is nasal patency tested?","choices":["Ask the patient to blow the nose and listen for asymmetry","Hold a mirror under the nose and compare the two areas of condensation","Transilluminate each nostril in a dark room","Occlude one nostril and ask the patient to breathe in"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each side is tested separately, because unilateral obstruction is what carries the worrying differential. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are exostoses, and which type of hearing loss do they cause?","choices":["Benign bony growths in the ear canal, causing conductive loss","Benign bony growths on the ossicles, causing sensorineural loss","Malignant bone tumours of the temporal bone","Cholesterol deposits in the middle ear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They narrow the canal mechanically, which is why the loss is conductive. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A normal Rinne on the right, and the Weber lateralises to the right. Where is the lesion and what type is it?","choices":["Sensorineural loss in the right ear","Conductive loss in the left ear","Sensorineural loss in the LEFT ear","Conductive loss in the right ear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A normal Rinne rules out conductive loss on the side tested, and Weber lateralising right places the sensorineural damage on the left. This was the worked case put to the class. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which headache patterns are EPISODIC, and which are UNILATERAL?","choices":["All three are episodic and all three unilateral","Only cluster is episodic and only tension unilateral","Migraine and tension are episodic; migraine and cluster are unilateral","Migraine and cluster are episodic; migraine and tension are unilateral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Migraine appears in both lists, which is why the two features have to be taken together rather than either alone. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"How does the nasal mucosa look in VIRAL rhinitis compared with ALLERGIC rhinitis?","choices":["Viral is pale and bluish; allergic is red and swollen","Both are identical and cannot be distinguished","Viral is ulcerated; allergic is bleeding","Viral is red and swollen; allergic is pale, bluish or red"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pale bluish appearance is the one that points to allergy, and it is easy to miss if you are only looking for redness. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How long does benign positional vertigo last overall, as opposed to how long a single episode lasts?","choices":["Episodes and the condition both last a few weeks","Each episode is under a minute, but the condition lasts a few weeks and may recur","Each episode is under a minute, and the condition resolves within 24 hours","Episodes last hours, and the condition lasts a few weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The table separates the two timescales, and confusing them is the usual error. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
